<td id="S3.T1.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.7.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">64</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.7.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#B30000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.7.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.8" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.7.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">World</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">OpenEgo (ours)</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">1107</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">119.6M</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">290</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">344.5k</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.8.8" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.2pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.8.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Camera</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Egocentric datasets combined to form <span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenEgo</span>. “Fine-Grained Annotations” refers to temporal action segments; “Dexterous Annotations” refers to 3D multi-finger/hand-pose labels. The final column reports the source coordinate frame of hand-pose annotations. Distances (where applicable) are measured in meters.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview.</span>
OpenEgo consolidates six egocentric sources into 1107 hours, 119.6M frames, 290 tasks, and 344.5k recordings (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 OpenEgo Dataset ‣ OpenEgo: A Large-Scale Multimodal Egocentric Dataset for Dexterous Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Environments span 10 kitchens and 610 indoor rooms, include 24 cooking recipes and 1.4k distinct objects, and involve at least 258 unique participants. The dataset contains both short- and long-form demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hand joints.</span>
We align all hand poses to MANO’s 21-joint layout (4 joints per finger plus wrist) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and express final coordinates in the <em id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">camera</em> frame. For sources lacking dexterous labels (CaptainCook4D), we detect 2D landmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and back-project using dataset intrinsics and per-pixel depth from the RGB-D stream <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Given pixel <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=(u,v,1)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=(u,v,1)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, depth <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, and intrinsics <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, we recover camera-frame 3D by</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{c}\;=\;z\,\mathbf{K}^{-1}\,\tilde{\mathbf{u}},\quad\tilde{\mathbf{u}}=(u,v,1)^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{c}\;=\;z\,\mathbf{K}^{-1}\,\tilde{\mathbf{u}},\quad\tilde{\mathbf{u}}=(u,v,1)^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">When a dataset provides world-frame hand pose and calibrated extrinsics, we convert to the camera frame via the rigid transform supplied per frame:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{c}\;=\;\mathbf{R}\,\mathbf{X}_{w}+\mathbf{t},\qquad(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t})\text{ from dataset extrinsics}\cite[cite]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{holoassist,hot3d,hocap}{\@@citephrase{(}}{\@@citephrase{)}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> from dataset extrinsics</mtext><mtext class="ltx_citemacro_cite"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite></mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{c}\;=\;\mathbf{R}\,\mathbf{X}_{w}+\mathbf{t},\qquad(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{t})\text{ from dataset extrinsics}\cite[cite]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{holoassist,hot3d,hocap}{\@@citephrase{(}}{\@@citephrase{)}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Per-dataset pipelines are as follows (all outputs are MANO-21 joints in camera frame):</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CaptainCook4D</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>: no native hand pose; detect 2D landmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and back-project with depth to obtain 3D joints (camera frame).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HOI4D</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>: uses released MANO parameters; we obtain MANO keypoints in the provided camera frame. For frames without poses, we fall back to landmark detection and depth back-projection as above <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HoloAssist</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>: hand poses are provided in the world frame; we convert to the camera frame using per-frame camera extrinsics supplied by the dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EgoDex</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>: hand pose is in the camera frame; we map their 25-joint format to MANO-21 by dropping the four non-MANO joints and reindexing to MANO ordering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HOT3D</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>: MANO hand pose is defined in the world frame; we retrieve joints and transform to the camera frame using the dataset’s per-frame extrinsics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HO-Cap</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>: MANO parameters are provided; we compute MANO keypoints and convert world-to-camera using the frame-wise extrinsics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">For all sources, we provide a binary visibility mask and retain missing joints when occluded or absent, following the dataset’s sensing and annotations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Language primitives.</span>
We provide intention-aligned language annotations consisting of descriptive action primitives. Each primitive specifies the manipulated object and action, with absolute timestamps (<span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t_start</span>, <span id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t_end</span>) from intention onset to completion. For example, primitives include <span id="S3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘navigate to desk’’</span>, <span id="S3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘remove GoPro camera from case’’</span>, or <span id="S3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘right hand unzips black camera case while left hand holds it’’</span>. Manipulation entries include actor labels (<span id="S3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">left_hand</span>, <span id="S3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">right_hand</span>, <span id="S3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">both_hands</span>); navigation entries use <span id="S3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">person</span> with a destination/object. Objects are named directly when identifiable, or described by attributes when uncertain. We annotate only directly observed actions and retain gaps between them. We also provide high-level task labels. This process is applied across all sources, including those with existing fine-grained annotations when descriptions differ or are incomplete <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem setup.</span> Each frame contains up to two hands with a unified 21-joint layout per hand (4 per finger plus wrist). We represent the state at time <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as stacked left/right hand joints <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{42\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{42\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> in the camera frame, with <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates, and a binary visibility mask <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}\in\{0,1\}^{42}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>42</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}\in\{0,1\}^{42}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given an RGB observation <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, a language prompt <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> describing the intended manipulation, and the current joints <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the policy predicts the next <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> actions <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{q}_{t+1:t+T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}_{t+1:t+T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the prediction horizon used for training and evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Policy and training.</span>
We adopt a ViLT policy following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. The model is trained with a masked mean-squared error objective,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\;=\;\frac{1}{\sum_{\tau=1}^{T}\sum_{j}m_{t+\tau,j}}\sum_{\tau=1}^{T}\sum_{j}m_{t+\tau,j}\,\big\lVert\hat{q}_{t+\tau,j}-q_{t+\tau,j}\big\rVert_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\;=\;\frac{1}{\sum_{\tau=1}^{T}\sum_{j}m_{t+\tau,j}}\sum_{\tau=1}^{T}\sum_{j}m_{t+\tau,j}\,\big\lVert\hat{q}_{t+\tau,j}-q_{t+\tau,j}\big\rVert_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which ignores error on joints marked invisible by <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to computational limits, we train on a 0.1% subset of OpenEgo and holding out 10% of demonstrations for evaluation to avoid leakage. We optimize for 15,000 gradient steps using the AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> optimizer and cosine annealing learning rate schedule with batch size 896 (<math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>13.44M sampled training instances) on 2 <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs. We follow the training framework of prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span>
We report sequence-prediction quality on the held-out split using only joints marked visible. We use three standard trajectory metrics: (i) <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AED</span> — average Euclidean distance over the horizon; (ii) <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FED</span> — Euclidean distance at the final step; and (iii) <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DTW</span> — dynamic time-warping distance between predicted and reference joint sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ OpenEgo: A Large-Scale Multimodal Egocentric Dataset for Dexterous Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports sequence prediction across horizons. Shorter horizons yield lower average and final errors; both AED and FED increase with horizon. DTW grows more steeply, reflecting sensitivity to timing misalignment. DTW grows faster than AED/FED because timing offsets accumulate over longer horizons, increasing alignment cost. The evaluation shows that models trained on OpenEgo can learn short-horizon dexterous motion effectively, with errors increasing smoothly as the prediction horizon extends. This indicates the dataset supplies a structured learning signal, where task difficulty scales smoothly with the prediction horizon.</p>
</div>
